PART 3 — CRITICAL AUTHENTICATION AND SECURITY FIXES
==================================================

SECURITY ISSUE A: PUBLIC ROLE ESCALATION

Inspect public registration in server/routes.ts.

The current registration logic accepts a role from the request body if it matches a valid role.

This is unsafe because public users must not be able to register as PI_LEAD, REVIEWER or AUDITOR.

Implement:

- Public registration always creates a RESEARCHER account or a limited pending account.
- Ignore all privileged role values supplied by the client.
- Only an authorized PI_LEAD can assign or change project membership roles.
- Verify the PI has permission to manage that specific project.
- Prevent unauthorized role escalation through direct API requests.
- Record membership changes in the audit log.
- Add regression tests for every privileged role.
- Confirm that the frontend cannot bypass backend authorization.

SECURITY ISSUE B: CAMERA AUTHENTICATION

Inspect the camera authentication endpoint in server/routes.ts and session validation in server/auth.ts.

The existing implementation accepts a client-provided cameraConfirmed boolean.

A client-provided boolean must NEVER be treated as proof of identity.

Implement:

- Remove camera login as an independent authentication method.
- Require normal password-based authentication before sensitive authentication.
- Do not create authenticated sessions solely from a cameraConfirmed field.
- Do not allow the client to set camera_verified directly.
- Do not treat getUserMedia permission as biometric identity verification.
- If retaining the camera preview, make it an optional local-only presence preview.
- Do not upload or store camera footage.
- Add tests proving that a forged cameraConfirmed request cannot authenticate another user.
- Update all UI labels to describe the camera feature truthfully.

SECURITY ISSUE C: SESSION MANAGEMENT

Review the complete session lifecycle.

Implement and verify:

- Cryptographically secure session identifiers.
- HttpOnly session cookies.
- Secure cookies in production HTTPS.
- Appropriate SameSite settings.
- Session expiry.
- Logout invalidation.
- Session rotation after authentication and privilege changes.
- CSRF protection for all state-changing operations.
- Server-side session invalidation.
- No session tokens in URLs.
- No passwords or bearer tokens in browser logs.
- No session secrets in API error messages.
- Correct behavior after browser refresh.
- Correct behavior after expired sessions.
- Protection against session fixation.

Do not break the existing AI Studio iframe compatibility mechanism unless you provide an equally secure alternative.

SECURITY ISSUE D: RATE LIMITING

Inspect all authentication and sensitive routes.

Implement:

- Login rate limiting.
- Registration rate limiting.
- Sensitive-action rate limiting.
- Upload rate limiting.
- Re-authentication rate limiting.
- Trusted proxy configuration.
- Do not trust arbitrary X-Forwarded-For headers.
- Do not permit public clients to disable rate limiting.
- Do not expose test-only rate-limit overrides in production.
- Return appropriate HTTP 429 responses.
- Provide a useful retry message without exposing internal security details.

